---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/black-friday';
const title = 'Black Friday SMS campaigns: offers, queues and recovery | Shopify SMS Marketing Apps';
const desc = 'Plan Black Friday SMS around permission, offer validity, inventory, service capacity and incremental contribution rather than a fixed broadcast schedule.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/use-cases" class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-700 hover:text-slate-950">← All use cases</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Black Friday campaigns</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Run Black Friday SMS against a live operating plan</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">A sale calendar is only a proposal. Before each release, confirm who can receive the offer, whether checkout still honors it, and whether stock, fulfillment and support can absorb the next wave.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Explore app evaluations</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/offer-discipline">Offer discipline</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Define the audience before reserving a slot</h2>
      <p>Give each campaign an owner, offer ID, audience definition and cancellation deadline. Retain channel-specific permission evidence: capture source, time, disclosure version and any withdrawal. A prior purchase, email subscription or VIP label alone should not authorize promotional SMS. Keep uncertain identities and missing permission evidence out of the send queue until resolved; do not infer a shared person's permission from a matching household address.</p>
      <p>Recheck eligibility at dispatch, including withdrawal, destination, purchase state, support exclusions and experiment assignment. Scope purchase exits to the offer: a buyer who already used this offer should not receive its checkout reminder. A different order does not automatically explain whether another promotion is relevant. Define that rule explicitly. Preserve each channel's withdrawal scope and apply broader exclusions where requested; never use another sender or channel to bypass a withdrawal.</p>
      <p>Choose destination-specific sending windows with qualified policy review. Record the recipient timezone and its evidence, handle daylight-saving changes, and hold ambiguous locations. A store clock or phone prefix alone is a weak basis for a deadline promise. See <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">permission controls</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">sending windows</a>; this operating plan does not establish legal compliance.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Version the offer and its clock</h2>
      <p>Freeze a versioned record of eligible products and variants, customer restrictions, markets, currency, minimum spend, stacking rules, start and expiry instants, timezone shown to shoppers, and exclusions. Test the landing page, message and checkout against the same version. Specify whether a deadline governs checkout completion or another action; a cart created before expiry need not preserve a discount.</p>
      <p>Store timestamps as unambiguous instants and display the relevant local zone. A recipient-window delay must not turn an evening offer into an expired morning message. At dispatch, require enough remaining validity for the intended action under the team's chosen policy. If an offer is extended, create a new approved version and update every surface; do not silently recycle an old countdown or imply a false final chance.</p>
      <p>Keep inventory promises at the variant, market and fulfillment-location level. Shopify distinguishes <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/inventory-and-locations/fundamentals/inventory-states">Available inventory from On hand, Committed, Unavailable and Incoming inventory</a>: incoming stock is not available to sell, and on-hand totals include stock that cannot currently be sold. Use current sellable availability for the relevant destination, plus the merchant's safety buffer. Do not interpret a notification as a reservation or assume zero-stock selling is permitted.</p>
      <p>Recheck stock and offer validity after every queue delay. Remove sold-out variants from the destination, stop their pending jobs, and avoid a product-level scarcity claim when only one size is scarce. Concurrent purchases can still exhaust stock after a send; define the customer-facing sold-out and recovery path before launching.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Arbitrate collisions across the entire weekend</h2>
      <p>Put Black Friday, Cyber Monday, welcome, cart, loyalty and back-in-stock jobs on one contact-level calendar across tools. Include email where it repeats the same promise. Resolve a collision by the customer's current state and the offer's purpose, rather than letting every workflow claim a separate allowance.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Protect necessary order and service communications from promotional backlog; their content and permission basis still need review. Keep promotional additions out of a service message used to bypass a cap.</li>
        <li>Suppress this offer's reminders after a qualifying purchase. Route an unresolved delivery problem to service rather than another urgency message.</li>
        <li>Choose one owner for an overlapping cart discount and Black Friday offer. Apply the approved stacking rule at checkout and remove contradictory queued copy.</li>
        <li>Set channel and combined contact limits from the audience's expectations, prior exposure and observed response. Do not refill a cancelled slot automatically or impose one universal weekend cadence.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Use an atomic contact-level reservation or equivalent coordination so simultaneous workers cannot both pass a stale exposure check. Reserve against an offer version and purpose, and release cancelled reservations deliberately. If tools cannot share this control, reduce overlapping automation or route dispatch through one owner. <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">Email–SMS coordination</a> covers the shared calendar.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Release waves only while capacity permits</h2>
      <p>Estimate dispatch throughput, expected checkout demand, warehouse throughput and support staffing separately. A provider accepting a job does not mean the recipient has received it, and a fast send queue does not prove fulfillment capacity. Reserve service capacity and start with a bounded promotional wave sized to the tightest operational constraint.</p>
      <p>Assign an incident owner who can pause new promotion intake, queued dispatch and automated retries across every sender. Define thresholds for stale inventory, checkout failures, discount mismatches, delivery backlog, opt-outs and support load before release. Use a hard expiry for jobs so a backlog cannot become a late-night or post-sale burst. Document which jobs a pause can cancel and which provider-accepted messages cannot be recalled.</p>
      <p>After a pause, reconcile accepted work before resuming. Recheck permission, windows, offer version, purchaser exits and stock on the remaining queue; discard stale jobs rather than draining it to meet a volume target. Resume a small wave only after the owner confirms recovery. If checkout is broken, a promotional correction may add load: decide whether a service response, updated destination or cancellation best helps affected customers.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Handle late events and uncertain retries</h2>
      <p>Record event time, ingestion time, source ID and version. An order arriving late should cancel an unsent reminder even if it predates the latest import. Older offer updates must not overwrite a newer expiry or resurrect a disabled campaign. Apply withdrawal independently of marketing event order, and reconcile missed events against the authoritative order and permission records.</p>
      <p>Use a durable dispatch ledger keyed to contact, offer version and message purpose. If a provider times out, mark the attempt uncertain and reconcile its request ID or status before retrying. Do not treat missing delivery confirmation as proof that no send occurred. Where status cannot be resolved, hold for investigation instead of creating a duplicate urgency message.</p>
      <p>Replay recovered events without automatically dispatching every historical job. Rebuild current eligibility first, then admit only still-useful, unexpired work. Keep an audit of cancelled, accepted, failed and uncertain attempts so support can explain a customer's experience without inventing delivery or purchase causality.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Price the offer in contribution, then test incrementality</h2>
      <p>Use net merchandise revenue after discounts and refunds, less product cost, fulfillment, shipping subsidy, payment costs, variable messaging charges and incremental service costs. Do not subtract a discount again when revenue already reflects it. Separate fixed platform and setup costs for the rollout decision; include gift or bundle costs even when the headline offer is not a percentage discount.</p>
      <p>Hypothetical worksheet: a $100 basket discounted by $20 leaves $80 net merchandise revenue before returns. With $35 product cost, $10 fulfillment and shipping subsidy, $3 payment cost and $2 messaging and service allocation, contribution is $30. The equivalent full-price basket with the same $50 costs contributes $50. Matching that contribution would require 50 ÷ 30, about 1.67 times as many orders under these assumptions, before additional fixed costs or capacity changes. This is a break-even comparison, not a forecast.</p>
      <p>Randomly assign eligible customers before release to the proposed campaign and a holdout. Keep assignment stable across overlapping weekend jobs and record which other communications both arms receive. Specify whether the question concerns the extra SMS, the discount, or the whole campaign; a no-SMS holdout receiving the same email offer cannot isolate the discount's effect.</p>
      <p>Compare total matured contribution per assigned eligible customer in each arm, including non-buyers, over the same prespecified window. Report the difference, uncertainty, sample size, refunds, opt-outs and service costs. Attributed revenue is descriptive rather than proof of lift. Scarce inventory creates interference when one arm exhausts stock for the other; report that limitation, and avoid extrapolating a small wave to the whole weekend. A noisy result may justify retaining uncertainty rather than declaring a winner.</p>
      <p>Use <a class="link" href="/use-cases/analytics">campaign measurement</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a> to plan the worksheet before choosing volume.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Hypothetical scenarios and proposed acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>These are proposed behaviors to test in your own stack, not merchant results or promises that an app implements them.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>VIP with email permission only:</strong> an eligible VIP has no promotional SMS evidence. Expected: exclude from SMS; evaluate email separately against its permission and exposure rules.</li>
        <li><strong>Offer expires during a sending-window hold:</strong> a queued message becomes eligible to send after its offer expires. Expected: cancel it, with no automatic replacement or deadline extension.</li>
        <li><strong>One variant sells out:</strong> the advertised size is no longer sellable in the recipient's market. Expected: stop that variant's pending jobs; do not substitute another size or claim a reserved item.</li>
        <li><strong>Two offers race:</strong> a cart worker and weekend broadcast reserve the same contact simultaneously. Expected: one approved purpose wins under the collision rule, and checkout matches the surviving offer.</li>
        <li><strong>Support backlog crosses the pause threshold:</strong> the incident owner stops promotional dispatch while order service remains available. Expected: expired jobs are discarded on recovery and a bounded wave precedes broader resumption.</li>
        <li><strong>Late purchase and provider timeout:</strong> a qualifying order arrives while a reminder attempt has uncertain acceptance. Expected: cancel unsent work, reconcile the attempt, and prevent a blind retry; record any message already accepted.</li>
        <li><strong>Old offer event arrives after an extension:</strong> an earlier version would restore the old terms. Expected: reject the stale update and keep copy, destination and checkout on the approved current version.</li>
        <li><strong>High attributed sales, uncertain holdout lift:</strong> small groups and shared inventory make the contribution difference imprecise. Expected: report uncertainty and inventory interference, without claiming incremental success.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Evaluate tools against the operating plan</h2>
      <p>If a dedicated SMS workflow fits your staffing and data model, use the <a class="link" href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> and <a class="link" href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a> evaluations as starting points for a demonstration of queue cancellation, permission updates and uncertain-send recovery. If one team owns email and SMS, compare <a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> and <a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> against the same collision fixtures. For a separate email workflow, include <a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> in the evaluation and demonstrate coordination with the selected sender.</p>
      <p>Require evidence for the exact plan, destination and integration you would use: current throughput limits, billing units, cancellation boundaries, event latency, operator access and recovery procedures. Assign an owner for controls that require external coordination. Choose the stack that passes your required scenarios within budget; vendor labels or a sales-volume tier do not establish that fit.</p>
      <p>Before releasing the next wave, the owner should be able to name the current offer version, eligible audience, remaining validity, stock authority, collision winner, capacity threshold, stop control and measurement assignment. If one is unknown, hold that wave. Continue with <a class="link" href="/use-cases/back-in-stock">stock-triggered campaigns</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> for related operating decisions.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
